
"smart_sources:2026-05-25 - 'Going green now for who_’ Yakama protest clean energy project on sacred site to power data center • Idaho Capital Sun.md": {"path":"2026-05-25 - 'Going green now for who_’ Yakama protest clean energy project on sacred site to power data center • Idaho Capital Sun.md","last_embed":{"hash":null},"embeddings":{},"last_read":{"hash":"3tmhmf","at":1785795032970},"class_name":"SmartSource","last_import":{"mtime":1783546060561,"size":13780,"at":1785795033069,"hash":"3tmhmf"},"blocks":{"#---frontmatter---":[1,8],"##‘Going green now for who?’ Yakama protest clean energy project on sacred site to power data center":[9,52],"##‘Going green now for who?’ Yakama protest clean energy project on sacred site to power data center#{1}":[11,52],"##Power for who?":[53,83],"##Power for who?#{1}":[55,83]},"outlinks":[{"title":"The Columbia River Gorge as seen from above on May 8, 2026, near Pushpum, a site sacred to the Yakama Nation. (Photo by Alex Baumhardt/Oregon Capital Chronicle)","target":"https://idahocapitalsun.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/DSC06936-1024x680-1.jpg","line":13,"embedded":true},{"title":"issued key permits","target":"https://washingtonstatestandard.com/2026/02/02/feds-greenlight-2b-renewable-energy-project-on-yakama-nation-sacred-site/","line":23},{"title":"finding","target":"https://web.archive.org/web/20221221210457/https:/apps.ecology.wa.gov/publications/documents/2206015.pdf","line":23},{"title":"fighting","target":"https://www.columbiariverkeeper.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Columbia-Riverkeeper-Rehearing-Request.pdf","line":23},{"title":"Elaine Harvey, a member of the Yakama Nation and a watershed manager at the Columbia River Inter-Tribal Fish Commission speaks at an event near Pushpum, a site sacred to Columbia River tribes near Goldendale, Wash. on Friday, May 8, 2026. Leaders from the Yakama Nation, as well as nonprofit environmental groups, are hoping to stop the development of a pumped storage energy project which they say will threaten the site, the seeds and roots tribal members harvest from it as well as wetlands, groundwater and wildlife in the area. (Photo by Alex Baumhardt/Oregon Capital Chronicle)","target":"https://idahocapitalsun.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/DSC06904-scaled-1.jpg","line":33,"embedded":true},{"title":"Northwest Public Broadcasting","target":"https://www.nwpb.org/local/2026-05-01/part-of-an-old-aluminum-smelter-along-the-columbia-river-could-soon-get-cleaned-up-an-energy-project-is-helping-fast-track-the-work","line":45},{"title":"Street Roots","target":"https://www.streetroots.org/news-stories/2026/05/20/its-not-built-yet-yakama-nation-fights-3-3b-energy-project-and-data-center-development-on-sacred-site/","line":45},{"title":"People gather near Pushpum, a site sacred to Columbia River tribes near Goldendale, Wash. on Friday, May 8, 2026. Leaders from the Yakama Nation, as well as nonprofit environmental groups, are hoping to stop the development of a pumped storage energy project which they say will threaten the site, the seeds and roots tribal members harvest from it as well as wetlands, groundwater and wildlife in the area. (Photo by Alex Baumhardt/Oregon Capital Chronicle)","target":"https://idahocapitalsun.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/DSC06944-scaled-1.jpg","line":49,"embedded":true},{"title":"recent projections","target":"https://app.leg.wa.gov/ReportsToTheLegislature/Home/GetPDF?fileName=Washington+State+Electric+Utility+Resource+Planning+2024+Report_FINAL_2be3ab47-13c7-45fc-a113-808ee29dbc69.pdf","line":61},{"title":"letter","target":"https://www.ryedevelopment.com/assets/documents/goldendale/FLA/Goldendale-FLA_Appendix-M_Letters-in-Support.pdf","line":63},{"title":"Youth from the Yakama Nation perform the Swan Dance near Pushpum, a site sacred to Columbia River tribes near Goldendale, Wash. on Friday, May 8, 2026. Leaders from the Yakama Nation, as well as nonprofit environmental groups, are hoping to stop the development of a pumped storage energy project which they say will threaten the site, the seeds and roots tribal members harvest from it as well as wetlands, groundwater and wildlife in the area. (Photo by Alex Baumhardt/Oregon Capital Chronicle)","target":"https://idahocapitalsun.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/DSC06882-scaled-1.jpg","line":73,"embedded":true},{"title":"Oregon Capital Chronicle","target":"https://oregoncapitalchronicle.com/2026/05/25/going-green-now-for-who-yakama-protest-clean-energy-project-on-sacred-site-to-power-data-center/","line":83}],"metadata":{"title":"'Going green now for who?’ Yakama protest clean energy project on sacred site to power data center • Idaho Capital Sun","source":"https://idahocapitalsun.com/2026/05/25/repub/going-green-now-for-who-yakama-protest-clean-energy-project-on-sacred-site-to-power-data-center/?utm_source=newsletters.idahocapitalsun.com&utm_medium=newsletter&utm_campaign=isp-transports-more-immigrants-to-ice-record-visitation-at-minidoka-column-rebuilding-civics-participation&_bhlid=fb42505c252203cdcb04f0933d33e2e21fcd63e5","author":null,"published":"2026-05-25","created":"2026-07-08","description":"Mounting evidence shows a clean energy project in Washington on a Yakama sacred site would in large part power a data center."},"task_lines":[],"tasks":{},"codeblock_ranges":[]},